      ],
      "source": "https://oregon.com/recreation/neahkahnie-mountain-0"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The beach below Neahkahnie Mountain might be where Europeans first set foot in Oregon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://oregon.com/recreation/neahkahnie-mountain-0"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sam Reed founded the area's first oceanfront hotel and drinking establishment, the Neahkahnie Tavern and Inn.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitmanzanita.org/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The British ship Glenesslin ran aground on the rocks below Neahkahnie Mountain in 1913.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitmanzanita.org/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Real estate agents touted Neahkahnie as the Carmel of the North by 1914 to attract Portland residents.",
